## 5. The decision this plan exists to force: the TUI status bar

`Tui#render_status` (tui.rb:170) draws a persistent bar:

```
 boukensha v0.11.0 · claude-haiku-4-5  ·  ctx 1.2k  ·  26 tools  ·  14:32:07
```

The tool count changes on its own when MCP registers 26 tools. **The route does
not appear anywhere persistent.**

Step 10 taught exactly this lesson the hard way: a banner that described the
switched-off route read as "not configured" while 26 working tools sat in the
registry, and that misleading signal was what exposed a *second*, silent bug.
The TUI has the same exposure and worse ergonomics — the banner scrolls away
after the first exchange, while the status bar is always on screen.

| Option | Trade-off |
| --- | --- |
| **A. Leave it.** Banner only. | Zero TUI change. But the only persistent readout never says whether the MUD is in-process, over MCP, or absent — and the banner is gone within one screenful. |
| **B. Add the route to the status bar** — `· mcp` or `· mud:direct`. | ~4 lines in `render_status`. The bar is width-constrained and already has five fields; something may need shortening on a narrow terminal. |
| **C. Status bar shows connection health**, not just route. | Most useful, most work — needs the daemon polled or a cached last-known state. Out of scope here. |

**Recommendation: B.** It is small, and the step-10 experience is direct
evidence that the cost of an ambiguous status readout is measured in hours of
misdiagnosis, not in pixels. Reuse `Repl#mud_status_string` rather than
re-deriving — one source of truth for "which route is live".

